{"id":"superseded-gift-donors","title":"Organizations whose gifts the published list lost, and what the department pays them","question":"Among the gifts on replaced filings that the public list does not carry, which come from organizations with contracts, permits or other business with the department?","finding":"69 organization-and-department pairs gave $2.5 million on the replaced filings; 389 gifts worth $381,080 from individuals are counted and not named. One apparent loss was not a loss: Public Health's original February 2025 filing listed 4 gifts from Epic Systems Corporation, $393,750 in all, that an amendment removed because they still needed the Board's approval; the list carries them under May 2025 and June 2025, after the Board accepted them by Resolutions 238-25, 239-25, 290-25 and 291-25. In all the list shows 12 gifts from Epic to Public Health, $879,251; the department paid Epic State and Local Government $57.2 million since fiscal 2023. Another Planet Entertainment, which holds the Outside Lands use permit, gave Recreation and Park festival tickets and passes worth $72,855 (Aug 2026), answering \"Yes\" to a contract, an issued permit and a financial interest; none of these items is in the list. The Sheriff's December 2025 original lists the donor of $36,894 for first-responder events as the Sheriff himself, the official who signed the form; the November gift for a multi-jurisdictional training exercise came from the Sheriff's Foundation.","how":"Gifts from the superseded-gift-filings screen that are on no filing the list carries, kept when the donor name reads as an organization or the department answered \"Yes\" to a contract or financial interest, and grouped by donor and department. Payments: the receiving department's payments to the same name (letters only) since fiscal 2023, and all City payments since fiscal 2019; Epic is matched through the contract the form itself names.","limits":"Name matches. A gift from a contractor or permit holder is 